.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_DISABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_disable_sending \- turn off sends on a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_circq_disable_sending(ccol_circular_queue *cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function marks the queue as closed to new sends. It then wakes every
thread that blocks inside ccol_circq_send_zc() or
ccol_circq_timed_send_zc() and waits for free space. Each of those threads
checks the flag again when it wakes up. It then returns ccol_not_permitted
and does not put its message into the queue. Each one therefore keeps the
ownership of its own msg->data.

Every later send also returns ccol_not_permitted. This is true for
ccol_circq_send_zc(), ccol_circq_try_send_zc() and
ccol_circq_timed_send_zc(). It continues until a call to
ccol_circq_enable_sending().

A receive does not change. The messages that are already in the queue stay
there, and a receive still gets them in the normal way. This is what makes
this function the natural way to close a producer side and let a consumer
drain what is left.

The function also wakes every write-direction waiter on this queue. Such a
waiter is a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an
ccol_event_loop registration. Each of them scans again and sees the new
state. It therefore does not wait for room that no send can use.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue that gets sends turned off
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - The function turned sends off
ccol_invalid_args  - cq is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function wakes every blocked sender immediately with a broadcast.
ccol_circq_enable_sending() turns sends on again.
The function does not change a receive.
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_enable_sending (3),
